#: ../doc/animation.rst:44
msgid "Key frame Markers"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:44
msgid "Colorful icons line the bottom of the clip for every keyframe (`circle=Bézier`, `diamond=linear`, `square=constant`). Each icon matches the color of its clip, effect, or transition. The selected item's keyframe icons are shown brighter. Filtering the property list also filters these icons. Click any icon to jump the play-head and select its clip, effect, or transition without opening the Properties dock. Double-click the icon, or right-click and choose :guilabel:`Properties`, to open the dock. Drag an icon left or right to move the keyframe and fine‑tune your animation timing. The play-head moves to the keyframe's new position when you finish dragging."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:48
msgid "Key Frames"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:49
msgid "A keyframe remembers a property's value at a particular moment. For example, to make a clip gradually grow larger, you can set its scale at the beginning and choose a larger scale near the end. OpenShot calculates the change between those two keyframes during playback."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:53
msgid "With :ref:`auto_keyframes_ref` on (the default), move the playhead to the moment you want to change and adjust a property in the Properties dock. OpenShot adds a keyframe there, or updates it if one already exists. Move to another moment and adjust the value again to build your animation."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:57
msgid "A property turns green when the playhead is on one of its keyframes. Small markers along the bottom of the clip show where its keyframes are. Their shapes indicate how the values change between them: ``circle=Bézier, diamond=linear, square=constant``."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:61
msgid "Use the :guilabel:`Next Marker` and :guilabel:`Previous Marker` toolbar buttons to step through the selected item's keyframes. They follow whichever clip, effect, or transition is selected. When an effect is selected, navigation also stops at the start and end of its parent clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:65
msgid "To adjust the **interpolation mode**, right click on the small graph icon next to a property value."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:71
msgid "Key-frame Interpolation"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:73
msgid "Bézier"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:73
msgid "Interpolated values use a quadratic curve, and ease-in and ease-out. Icon: :guilabel:`Circle`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:74
msgid "Linear"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:74
msgid "Interpolated values are calculated linear (each step value is equal). Icon: :guilabel:`Diamond`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:75
msgid "Constant"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:75
msgid "Interpolated values stay the same until the next key frame, and jump to the new value. Icon: :guilabel:`Square`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:78
msgid "For more info on creating key frames for location, rotation, scale, shear, and origin, see :ref:`clip_transform_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:79
msgid "For more info on preset animations, see :ref:`clip_presets_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:80
msgid "For a full list of key frames, see :ref:`clip_properties_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:85
msgid "Auto Keyframes"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:86
msgid "Auto Keyframes helps you animate as you edit. Leave it on to record changes at the playhead as you move, resize, or adjust a clip. You can turn it on or off with the diamond button on the timeline toolbar. The same setting applies to edits in the Properties dock and to the transform handles in the preview."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:90
msgid "Auto Keyframes starts enabled. If you prefer to place keyframes yourself, turn off **Auto Keyframes on Startup** in **Preferences → Timeline**. OpenShot will start with it off next time, and you can still switch it on whenever you need it."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:94
msgid "Turn Auto Keyframes off when you want to adjust existing values without adding more keyframes. For example, if you have not animated a clip's size, you can resize it while viewing any part of the clip and keep that size throughout playback."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:98
msgid "If a property is already animated, your edit changes its keyframe at the playhead, or the nearest one to the left. Suppose a clip grows from small to large: editing its scale halfway through with Auto Keyframes off changes the starting scale and leaves the ending scale alone. To change a particular keyframe, move the playhead onto it before editing. Turning Auto Keyframes off does not remove your animation."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:103
msgid "Auto Keyframes only records on clips under the playhead. When the playhead is before or after a clip, editing it changes its nearest keyframe instead, so the whole clip changes without animating. When you edit several selected clips at once, each clip follows this rule on its own: clips under the playhead are keyframed at the playhead, and the others change without animating."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:108
msgid "While editing between keyframes, the preview shows the animation at the playhead. The Properties dock may show the earlier keyframe you are changing, so its value can differ from what you see in the preview. Hover over a numeric value to check which frame you are editing."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:112
msgid "You can also build an animation by placing keyframes yourself, with Auto Keyframes off:"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:114
msgid "Move the playhead to where you want the animation to begin."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:115
msgid "Insert a keyframe using the keyframe panel or the Properties context menu. This remembers the current value."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:116
msgid "Move to another moment and insert another keyframe."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:117
msgid "Adjust its value. OpenShot animates between the two keyframes."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:119
msgid "Repeat these steps to add more changes to the animation. See :ref:`split_clip_ref` for preparing shorter segments with their own starting keyframes."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:123
msgid "Timing"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:124
msgid "Changing how fast a clip plays is done with the :guilabel:`Time` property and the :guilabel:`Timing` tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:126
msgid "The :guilabel:`Speed` menu offers presets such as normal, fast, slow, freeze, reverse, and repeat. See details in :ref:`clip_time_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:127
msgid "The :guilabel:`Timing` tool lets you drag a clip’s edges to speed it up or slow it down. OpenShot adds the needed Time keyframes and **scales your other keyframes** so your animations stay aligned. Shorter clips play faster, longer clips play slower. See more: :ref:`clip_time_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:132
msgid "Repeating"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:133
msgid "To play a clip multiple times, use :guilabel:`Right-Click → Speed → Repeat`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:135
msgid ":guilabel:`Loop` repeats in one direction (forward or reverse)."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:136
msgid ":guilabel:`Ping-Pong` alternates direction (forward then backward, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:137
msgid ":guilabel:`Custom` can add a short pause between passes, speed up or slow down each pass, including keyframes."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:139
msgid "OpenShot writes the :guilabel:`Time` curve for you, and you can edit those keyframes just like any other. See more: :ref:`clip_time_repeat_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:143
msgid "Bézier Presets"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:144
msgid "When using a Bézier curve for animation, OpenShot includes more than 20 curve presets (which affect the shape of the curve). For example, **Ease-In** has a more gradual slope at the beginning, making an animation move slower at the beginning, and faster at the end. **Ease-In/Out (Back)** has a gradual beginning and ending, but actually goes past the expected value and then back (producing a bounce effect)."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:149
msgid "To choose a curve preset, right click on the small graph icon next to a key frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:156
msgid "Ken Burns Effect"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:157
msgid "The **Ken Burns effect** is a pan-and-zoom animation technique — named after documentary filmmaker Ken Burns — that brings still images or video clips to life with slow, deliberate camera movement. In OpenShot, use :guilabel:`Right-Click → Motion → Camera` presets for one-click Ken Burns animations (Zoom In, Zoom Out, Pan, Zoom & Pan with Auto Direction), or set keyframes on :guilabel:`Location X/Y` and :guilabel:`Scale X/Y` manually for full control. See :ref:`clip_presets_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:166
msgid "Image Sequences"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:167
msgid "If you have a sequence of similarly named images (such as, cat001.png, cat002.png, cat003.png, etc...), you can simply drag and drop one of them into OpenShot, and you will be prompted to import the entire sequence. OpenShot will playback these sequential images rapidly, as if they are frames in a video. The rate or speed in which these images are displayed is based on their frame rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:172
msgid "**NOTE:** Be sure your image sequence starts at 0 or 1, or you will likely receive an error when importing it into OpenShot. For example, if your sequence starts at cat222.png, or has missing images in the sequence, OpenShot will struggle to understand the sequence. An easy work-around is to renumber the images so they start with 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:178
msgid "To adjust the frame rate of the animation, right click and choose **File Properties** in the **Project Files** panel, and adjust the frame rate. Once you have set the correct frame rate, drag the animation onto the timeline."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:189
msgid "File Properties"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:189
msgid "Select an image sequence in the **Project Files** panel, right click and choose **File Properties**"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:190
msgid "Frame Rate"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/animation.rst:190
msgid "Adjust the frame rate of the animation. Typically, hand-drawn animations use 12 frames per second."
msgstr ""
